## 1. Scope

This reference covers dual wall adhesive lined heat shrink with 3:1 and 4:1 recovery ratios. The reason to choose a high ratio is rarely insulation. It is usually the number of sizes a harness family has to carry.

## 2. Construction

- Cross-linked polyolefin outer wall with a hot melt adhesive inner layer.
- Expanded at 3:1 or 4:1, so one supplied size covers a wider band of cable and connector diameters.
- Outer wall and adhesive layer are declared separately on the datasheet.
- Supplied in cut lengths, in a size-ordered kit, or on a reel.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shrink ratio | 3:1 and 4:1 | Higher ratio buys coverage range and costs wall thickness after recovery. |
| Continuous operating temperature | -55 C to +125 C (typical polyolefin) | Confirm against the specific grade datasheet. |
| Recovery | Hot air, typically 120 to 150 C air temperature | High ratio parts need even heating around the circumference. |
| Flame behaviour | UL 224 VW-1 (common specification) | A property of the grade rather than of the expansion ratio. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- The diameter band the part has to span, which is what justifies 3:1 or 4:1 in the first place.
- The wall thickness left after recovery at the small end, since a high ratio thins the wall furthest at the extremes.
- Whether the harness needs to stay flexible, because a recovered high ratio part is stiffer than a 2:1 part of the same material.
- Recovery temperature against nearby materials, especially connectors with a low temperature limit.
- The size list the harness family carries, which is where the real saving is.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- UL 224, extruded insulating tubing.
- SAE-AMS-DTL-23053 and the adhesive lined polyolefin slash sheet within that family.
- IEC 60684 series, flexible insulating sleeving.
- ASTM D2671, test methods for heat shrinkable tubing.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Recover evenly around the circumference. A high ratio part heated from one side shrinks on that side first and traps the rest.
- Allow for the adhesive to flow at both ends, which is the seal.
- Do not use a high ratio part as a substitute for a proper breakout boot where the geometry genuinely calls for one.
- Check the recovered result on an actual assembly, not on a straight length of cable.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Thin wall at the small end | A size chosen at the limit of its ratio range | Select so the target diameter sits comfortably inside the range rather than at the edge. |
| Adhesive not sealing at one end | Uneven heating left one end short of recovery | Heat around the circumference and continue until both ends have visibly flowed. |
| Harness too stiff after repair | 4:1 part used where a 2:1 part would have fitted | Use the lowest ratio that spans the diameter band. |
| Damaged connector during recovery | Recovery temperature above the connector limit | Set the air temperature from the lower of the two material limits. |

## 8. Typical applications

- EV and specialty vehicle harnesses with a wide spread of connector diameters.
- Truck, bus, marine and rail harnesses where size count is a logistics cost.
- Field repair where a single kit has to cover several splice sizes.
- Irregular connector bodies that a 2:1 part cannot pass over.

